.TH PING 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ping \- send ICMP and ICMPv6 echo requests to a host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ping
.RB [ \-46nqV ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR interval ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-W
.IR timeout ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR ttl ]
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ping
sends ICMP ECHO_REQUEST packets (ICMPv6 echo requests for IPv6) to
.I target
and reports each matching ECHO_REPLY, then prints summary statistics.
.PP
The address family is chosen from
.IR target :
a string containing a colon is treated as IPv6, anything else as IPv4,
unless
.B \-4
or
.B \-6
forces a family.
An IPv4
.I target
may be a dotted-quad address or a host name, which is resolved with
.BR gethostbyname (3);
the resolved address is printed first.
An IPv6
.I target
must be a literal address; host names are not resolved for IPv6.
.PP
Requests are sent every
.I interval
seconds regardless of replies, while the socket is polled for replies
between sends.
Each request carries the low 16 bits of the process ID as its
identifier and a sequence number starting at 1.
When the payload is at least 8 bytes, its first 8 bytes hold the send
time, from which the round-trip time is computed; the rest is filled
with an incrementing byte pattern.
Replies whose identifier does not match are ignored.
.PP
For each reply
.B ping
prints the reply size, source address, sequence number, round-trip
time and, for IPv4, the TTL of the received packet.
For IPv6 the address printed is the target address, not the source of
the reply.
.PP
.B ping
stops after
.I count
requests have been sent and a further
.I timeout
seconds have passed, or when it receives
.BR SIGINT .
It then prints the number of packets sent and received, the loss
percentage, the elapsed time and, if any reply arrived, the minimum,
average, maximum and standard deviation of the round-trip time.
.SS Privileges
The kernel allows
.B SOCK_RAW
sockets only to processes with effective user ID 0; others get
.BR EACCES .
The root filesystem build therefore installs
.B ping
set-user-ID root.
Immediately after the raw socket is created,
.B ping
sets its group and user IDs back to the real IDs; name resolution
and reply parsing run unprivileged.
.PP
On an IPv4 raw socket the kernel delivers the whole IP datagram,
including the IP header, and builds the outgoing IP header itself.
On an IPv6 raw socket it delivers only the ICMPv6 message.
Every raw ICMP socket receives every ICMP packet, which is why
replies are filtered by identifier.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-4
Use IPv4.
.TP
.B \-6
Use IPv6.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Stop after sending
.I count
requests.
A value of 0 or less, or omitting the option, means send until
interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-i " interval"
Wait
.I interval
seconds (a decimal fraction is allowed) between requests.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
Send
.I size
bytes of payload after the 8-byte ICMP header.
The default is 56.
Requests larger than 1500 bytes including the ICMP header are not
sent; each attempt is reported as a
.B sendto
error.
With a payload smaller than 8 bytes no timestamp is sent and
round-trip times are reported as 0.
.TP
.BI \-W " timeout"
After the last of
.I count
requests has been sent, wait
.I timeout
seconds for replies before stopping.
The default is 1.
It has no effect without
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI \-t " ttl"
Accepted but ignored; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: print only the banner and the summary.
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted for compatibility.
Addresses are always printed numerically.
.TP
.B \-V
Print version information and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message and exit with status 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one reply was received.
.TP
.B 1
No reply was received.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, an unresolvable target, failure to create the raw
socket, or failure to drop privileges.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ ping \-c 3 10.0.2.2
$ ping \-6 \-c 1 ::1
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gethostbyname_r (3),
.BR tcp (7)
.SH BUGS
.B \-t
is parsed but never applied to the socket, so requests are always sent
with the kernel default TTL (or hop limit) of 64.
.PP
The ICMPv6 checksum is left as zero.
Substrate's own stack answers such requests, but hosts that verify the
checksum discard them, so IPv6 pings to other systems get no reply.
.PP
For IPv4, a
.B sendto
failure is reported and
.B ping
keeps going; for IPv6 the first failure ends the run.
.PP
The numeric arguments are converted without error checking; a
malformed value is taken as 0.
